Chapter: Foreword--Capability

 This is where the truce ends. The table is here because the foreword is
 the only quire both sides may cite: Library Rule 2 fixes the order
 `codex.foreword -> codex -> codex.os`, so the compiler and the loader
 can each cite this and neither has to cite the other. A binary now gets
 the same authority whichever door it came in through, by construction
 rather than by a script that noticed when it did not.

 What this chapter deliberately does NOT do is derive the foreword
 `effect <Name> where` declarations. An effect declaration is a syntax
 form carrying its own operation signatures, and no table can produce
 one; `build/check-effect-vocab.ps1` guards that list and is the only
 instrument that ever could. That guard stays.

Section: Kernel Capability Bits

 The gaps are real. Bits 1, 2 and 13 are unassigned, and FileSystem and
 Network have no base bit at all -- they are read/write only, so a bare
 grant is the union of the two and never a third thing.

  cap-console : Integer = 0
  cap-concurrent : Integer = 3
  cap-console-read : Integer = 4
  cap-console-write : Integer = 5
  cap-filesystem-read : Integer = 6
  cap-filesystem-write : Integer = 7
  cap-network-read : Integer = 8
  cap-network-write : Integer = 9
  cap-block-device : Integer = 10
  cap-ipc : Integer = 11
  cap-process-create : Integer = 12
  cap-capability-admin : Integer = 14
  cap-identity : Integer = 15
  cap-device : Integer = 16
  cap-gpu-compute : Integer = 17
  cap-gpu-memory : Integer = 18
  cap-camera : Integer = 19
  cap-microphone : Integer = 20
  cap-location : Integer = 21
  cap-sensors : Integer = 22
  cap-display : Integer = 23
  cap-flash : Integer = 24
  cap-audio : Integer = 25
  cap-process : Integer = 26

Section: Directions

 The reason it mattered is that a drifted triple could not announce
 itself. `cap-dir-bits` below is the only reader of a direction anywhere,
 and its last branch is readwrite: a renumbered `write` does not fail a
 comparison, it falls through and expands into read AND write. The boot
 path and the verified-load path would have handed the same binary
 different authority, which is the failure this whole chapter exists to
 make unreachable.

  cap-dir-read : Integer = 0
  cap-dir-write : Integer = 1
  cap-dir-readwrite : Integer = 2

Section: The Table

 One row per capability, keyed by the identifier the CDX manifest
 carries. A row names its base bit, its read and write bits if the
 family has a direction, and any further bits the family implies.
 Negative one means the row has no such bit, which is a statement and
 not a placeholder: FileSystem has no base bit, and Concurrent has no
 direction.

 There are 18 rows and the type checker's vocabulary has 24 entries.
 The difference is not drift. Console, FileSystem and Network each admit
 a dotted refinement in an effect row (`[FileSystem.Read]`), and those
 six names are DERIVED below rather than stored, because a refinement is
 a direction on a capability and not a capability of its own.

 Process is the newest row and the reason it was missing is worth
 keeping. `[Process]` guards `get-args`, `get-env`, `current-dir`,
 `run-process`, `run-process-full` and `process-exit` -- host authority,
 and `run-process` in particular is arbitrary command execution, which
 is why it takes a bit of its own rather than borrowing Concurrent's
 `cap-process-create`. That bit is the bare-metal spawn authority and a
 different thing. Until this row existed the whole family was dead on
 arrival: every one of those builtins resolved and type-checked, and no
 program could declare the row needed to call any of them, because
 CDX4001 refused a name the vocabulary did not carry. Nothing in
 `codex/test` or `codex/os` declares `[Process]`, which is why an entire
 effect family being uncallable went unnoticed.

 Like Concurrent and Capability, Process has no `effect Process where`
 declaration in the foreword -- its operations are compiler builtins,
 typed in `Types/Builtins.codex` rather than by a declaration. That
 asymmetry is deliberate and `build/check-effect-vocab.ps1` carries it in
 its `capsWithoutEffect` list, so it is a recorded decision and not a
 silence.

  CapSpec = record {
   cs-name : Text,
   cs-id : Integer,
   cs-base-bit : Integer,
   cs-read-bit : Integer,
   cs-write-bit : Integer,
   cs-extra-bits : List Integer
  }

  capability-table : List CapSpec
  capability-table = [
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Console", cs-id = 0, cs-base-bit = cap-console, cs-read-bit = cap-console-read, cs-write-bit = cap-console-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "FileSystem", cs-id = 1, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = cap-filesystem-read, cs-write-bit = cap-filesystem-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Network", cs-id = 2, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = cap-network-read, cs-write-bit = cap-network-write, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Concurrent", cs-id = 3, cs-base-bit = cap-concurrent, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-ipc, cap-process-create] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Device", cs-id = 4, cs-base-bit = cap-block-device, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-device] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu.Compute", cs-id = 5, cs-base-bit = cap-gpu-compute, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu.Memory", cs-id = 6, cs-base-bit = cap-gpu-memory, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Identity", cs-id = 7, cs-base-bit = cap-identity, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Capability", cs-id = 8, cs-base-bit = cap-capability-admin, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Gpu", cs-id = 9, cs-base-bit = 0 - 1, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [cap-gpu-compute, cap-gpu-memory] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Camera", cs-id = 10, cs-base-bit = cap-camera, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Microphone", cs-id = 11, cs-base-bit = cap-microphone, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Location", cs-id = 12, cs-base-bit = cap-location, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Sensors", cs-id = 13, cs-base-bit = cap-sensors, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Display", cs-id = 14, cs-base-bit = cap-display, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Flash", cs-id = 15, cs-base-bit = cap-flash, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Audio", cs-id = 16, cs-base-bit = cap-audio, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] },
   CapSpec { cs-name = "Process", cs-id = 17, cs-base-bit = cap-process, cs-read-bit = 0 - 1, cs-write-bit = 0 - 1, cs-extra-bits = [] }
  ]

Section: The Expansion

  cap-or-bits : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-or-bits (bs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else cap-or-bits bs (i + 1) len (bit-or acc (bit-shl 1 (list-at bs i)))

  cap-dir-bits : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-dir-bits (dir) (read-bit) (write-bit) =
   if dir == cap-dir-read then bit-shl 1 read-bit
   else if dir == cap-dir-write then bit-shl 1 write-bit
   else bit-or (bit-shl 1 read-bit) (bit-shl 1 write-bit)

  cap-bits-for-spec : CapSpec, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-spec (spec) (dir) =
   let base = if spec.cs-base-bit >= 0 then bit-shl 1 (spec.cs-base-bit) else 0
   in let dirb = if spec.cs-read-bit >= 0 then cap-dir-bits dir (spec.cs-read-bit) (spec.cs-write-bit) else 0
   in let extra = cap-or-bits (spec.cs-extra-bits) 0 (list-length (spec.cs-extra-bits)) 0
   in bit-or base (bit-or dirb extra)

Section: Lookups

  cap-find-by-name : List CapSpec, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-find-by-name (ts) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ts i).cs-name == n then i
   else cap-find-by-name ts n (i + 1) len

  cap-find-by-id : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-find-by-id (ts) (id) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ts i).cs-id == id then i
   else cap-find-by-id ts id (i + 1) len

  cap-id-for-name : Text -> Integer
  cap-id-for-name (n) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-name ts n 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 - 1 else (list-at ts i).cs-id

  cap-bits-for-name : Text, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-name (n) (dir) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-name ts n 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 else cap-bits-for-spec (list-at ts i) dir

  cap-bits-for-id : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bits-for-id (id) (dir) =
   let ts = capability-table
   in let i = cap-find-by-id ts id 0 (list-length ts)
   in if i < 0 then 0 else cap-bits-for-spec (list-at ts i) dir

Section: The Vocabulary

 capability-table is a zero-parameter definition, so a mention of one is
 a call that rebuilds the table. Every function above binds it to a
 local exactly once for that reason. 18 rows is small enough that the
 rebuild does not matter at the rate these are called, which is per
 declared effect and not per emitted instruction -- but the shape is the
 same one that made emit-builtin expensive, so bind it, do not mention
 it twice.

  cap-names-from : List CapSpec, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  cap-names-from (ts) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let s = list-at ts i
   in let a1 = list-push acc (s.cs-name)
   in if s.cs-read-bit >= 0
      then cap-names-from ts (i + 1) len (list-push (list-push a1 (s.cs-name & ".Read")) (s.cs-name & ".Write"))
      else cap-names-from ts (i + 1) len a1

  capability-names : List Text
  capability-names =
   let ts = capability-table
   in cap-names-from ts 0 (list-length ts) []


Chapter: Math--Interval

 The shared interval-arithmetic kernel for the compiler's two range
 provers. The checker's aexpr-proven-range consumes it (through
 range-interval, TypeCheckerInference); the emitter's
 ir-expr-proven-range still carries a private, hand-written copy that
 refuses everything but non-negative operands, so on that side a named
 local holding a provably small signed value still has no provable
 range. This chapter is the single kernel both
 provers will consume, written for full signed i64 semantics and
 verified by an exhaustive containment test
 (codex/test/interval-exhaustive.codex) in the ConstantRange
 discipline: every transfer function states its soundness contract in
 prose, and the test checks that contract against the runtime's own
 operators for every interval over a small universe. The kernel is
 pure arithmetic over a two-field record; it allocates one record per
 call and nothing else.

Section: Representation

  Interval = record {
   lo : Integer,
   hi : Integer
  }

  iv-i64-max : Integer = 9223372036854775807

  iv-i64-min : Integer = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

  iv-unknown : Interval = Interval { lo = iv-i64-min, hi = iv-i64-max }

  iv-empty : Interval = Interval { lo = 1, hi = 0 }

  iv-is-unknown : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-unknown (a) = a.lo == iv-i64-min & a.hi == iv-i64-max

  iv-is-empty : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-empty (a) = a.lo > a.hi

  iv-exact : Integer -> Interval
  iv-exact (n) = Interval { lo = n, hi = n }

Section: Overflow Refusal

 Predicates that decide whether an i64 endpoint computation would
 overflow, without themselves overflowing. Addition uses the standard
 rearrangement: a + b overflows iff b > 0 and a > max - b, or b <= 0
 and a < min - b; both right-hand sides stay in range under their
 guards. Subtraction mirrors it. Multiplication compares against a
 truncating division of the endpoint constants - Codex integer
 division truncates toward zero, which is exactly the rounding these
 bounds need - after handling the two corners where either operand is
 -1 against i64-min, because negating i64-min is itself an overflow.

  iv-add-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-add-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max - b
   else a < iv-i64-min - b

  iv-sub-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-sub-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a < iv-i64-min + b
   else a > iv-i64-max + b

  iv-mul-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-mul-overflows (a) (b) =
   if a == 0 | b == 0 then False
   else if a == 0 - 1 then b == iv-i64-min
   else if b == 0 - 1 then a == iv-i64-min
   else if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max / b | a < iv-i64-min / b
   else a > iv-i64-min / b | a < iv-i64-max / b

  iv-int-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-min (a) (b) = if a <= b then a else b

  iv-int-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-max (a) (b) = if a >= b then a else b

Section: Addition

  iv-add : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-add (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-add-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-add-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo + b.lo, hi = a.hi + b.hi }

Section: Subtraction

  iv-sub : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-sub (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-sub-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-sub-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo - b.hi, hi = a.hi - b.lo }

Section: Negation

  iv-neg : Interval -> Interval
  iv-neg (a) =
   if iv-is-empty a then iv-empty
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0 - a.hi, hi = 0 - a.lo }

Section: Multiplication

  iv-mul : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mul (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else let p1 = a.lo * b.lo
   in let p2 = a.lo * b.hi
   in let p3 = a.hi * b.lo
   in let p4 = a.hi * b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min p1 p2) (iv-int-min p3 p4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max p1 p2) (iv-int-max p3 p4)
   }

Section: Division

  iv-div : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-div (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min & b.hi == 0 - 1 then iv-unknown
   else let q1 = a.lo / b.lo
   in let q2 = a.lo / b.hi
   in let q3 = a.hi / b.lo
   in let q4 = a.hi / b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min q1 q2) (iv-int-min q3 q4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max q1 q2) (iv-int-max q3 q4)
   }

Section: Modulo

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range, y in the other input's range, and y not zero, the
 concrete result int-mod x y lies inside the returned interval. What
 the runtime actually does (emit-int-mod-builtin in
 Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex): idiv leaves the truncated remainder,
 which carries the sign of the DIVIDEND, and a negative remainder is
 then corrected by adding the absolute value of the divisor. The
 result is therefore Euclidean - always in [0, abs(y) - 1] - for
 every sign combination, negative dividends and negative divisors
 included. The bound is [0, m] where m is the largest abs(y) - 1 over
 the divisor corners; abs(iv-i64-min) - 1 is exactly iv-i64-max, so
 the magnitude is computable at every corner. A divisor range
 containing zero answers iv-unknown, same argument as division.

  iv-mod-mag : Integer -> Integer
  iv-mod-mag (n) =
   if n == iv-i64-min then iv-i64-max
   else if n < 0 then (0 - n) - 1
   else n - 1

  iv-mod : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mod (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0, hi = iv-int-max (iv-mod-mag (b.lo)) (iv-mod-mag (b.hi)) }

Section: Union

  iv-union : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-union (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a & iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-is-empty a then b
   else if iv-is-empty b then a
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

Section: Intersection

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: every concrete x that lies in BOTH inputs'
 ranges lies inside the returned interval; the returned interval is
 in fact exactly the set of such x. Disjoint inputs produce the
 canonical iv-empty. An empty input needs no special case: taking the
 max of the lows and the min of the highs against any lo > hi pair
 yields lo > hi again, which normalizes to iv-empty.

  iv-intersect : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-intersect (a) (b) =
   let n-lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo)
   in let n-hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi)
   in if n-lo > n-hi then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = n-lo, hi = n-hi }

Section: Minimum and Maximum

  iv-min : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-min (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi) }

  iv-max : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-max (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

Section: Limitations

Page 1


Chapter: Parsmi--ListUtils

 We say:

Section: Construction
  list-zeros : Integer -> List Integer
  list-zeros (n) = list-zeros-loop n 0 []

  list-zeros-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  list-zeros-loop (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else list-zeros-loop n (i + 1) (list-push acc 0)

Section: Slicing
  list-tail : List a -> List a
  list-tail (xs) = list-tail-loop xs 1 (list-length xs) []

  list-tail-loop : List a, Integer, Integer, List a -> List a
  list-tail-loop (xs) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else list-tail-loop xs (i + 1) len (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

Section: Slicing (extended)

  list-take : List a, Integer -> List a
  list-take (xs) (n) = list-take-loop xs 0 (if n > list-length xs then list-length xs else n) []

  list-take-loop : List a, Integer, Integer, List a -> List a
  list-take-loop (xs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else list-take-loop xs (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  list-drop : List a, Integer -> List a
  list-drop (xs) (n) = list-tail-loop xs (if n > list-length xs then list-length xs else n) (list-length xs) []

Section: Map and Fold

  list-map : (a -> [e] b), List a -> [e] List b
  list-map (f) (xs) = map-list f xs

  map-list : (a -> [e] b), List a -> [e] List b
  map-list (f) (xs) =
   map-list-loop f xs 0 (list-length xs) []

  map-list-loop : (a -> [e] b), List a, Integer, Integer, List b -> [e] List b
  map-list-loop (f) (xs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else map-list-loop f xs (i + 1) len (list-push acc (f (list-at xs i)))

  fold-list : (b, a -> [e] b), b, List a -> [e] b
  fold-list (f) (z) (xs) =
   fold-list-loop f z xs 0 (list-length xs)

  fold-list-loop : (b, a -> [e] b), b, List a, Integer, Integer -> [e] b
  fold-list-loop (f) (z) (xs) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then z
   else fold-list-loop f (f z (list-at xs i)) xs (i + 1) len

Section: Filter and Search

  list-filter : List a, (a -> [e] Boolean) -> [e] List a
  list-filter (xs) (pred) =
    list-filter-loop xs pred 0 (list-length xs) []

  list-filter-loop : List a, (a -> [e] Boolean), Integer, Integer, List a -> [e] List a
  list-filter-loop (xs) (pred) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let x = list-at xs i
    in if pred x then list-filter-loop xs pred (i + 1) len (list-push acc x)
    else list-filter-loop xs pred (i + 1) len acc

  list-contains : List a, (a -> Boolean) -> Boolean
  list-contains (xs) (pred) =
    list-contains-loop xs pred 0 (list-length xs)

  list-contains-loop : List a, (a -> Boolean), Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  list-contains-loop (xs) (pred) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then False
    else if pred (list-at xs i) then True
    else list-contains-loop xs pred (i + 1) len

  list-remove : List a, (a -> Boolean) -> List a
  list-remove (xs) (pred) =
    list-remove-loop xs pred 0 (list-length xs) [] False

  list-remove-loop : List a, (a -> Boolean), Integer, Integer, List a, Boolean -> List a
  list-remove-loop (xs) (pred) (i) (len) (acc) (found) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let x = list-at xs i
    in if found then list-remove-loop xs pred (i + 1) len (list-push acc x) True
    else if pred x then list-remove-loop xs pred (i + 1) len acc True
    else list-remove-loop xs pred (i + 1) len (list-push acc x) False

